          { "band": "front", "width_cm": 380, "height_cm": 280 },
          { "band": "left", "width_cm": 780, "height_cm": 280 },
          { "band": "right", "width_cm": 780, "height_cm": 280 },
          { "band": "rear", "width_cm": 380, "height_cm": 280 },
          { "band": "top", "width_cm": 780, "height_cm": 380 }
        ],
        "sensor_height_cm": 170,
        "note": "The heavy tank of 16b cut AE.1: the stock tank's five sections, guns, sight and facet table, at 1.75 times its armour (700 against 400 on the front hull) and two thirds of its speed (1,000 cm/s against 1,500). At the 105's 120 a round, the front hull takes six rounds to strip and three more through its 320 internals, where the stock tank takes four and one. It starts, stops and turns slower too: 80 and 160 cm/s squared against 100 and 200, 20 degrees a second against 30, and 40 per cent in reverse against 50. The hull is 3.8 m by 7.8 m and 2.8 m tall, a bigger box than the tank's on every band, and the sight sits 1.7 m up. Five sections and not more, on purpose, so the damage pane fits it as it fits the tank. Both tracks carry the pair rule. VERSION 2 (16b cut AE.2): a six-round missile rack beside the cannon; FIRE missiles names it, the cannon stays the bare FIRE. Version 3 (17b cut M3.1): a command core in the hull. Version 4 (17b cut M3.6): losing the turret degrades the core."
